# A `Refuse` still ends the parse — the loop body's missing-`;` message is better
# than "expected" — but it comes last, after the slips met before it.

fn main() {
    let a = 1
    while (true) {
        f() g();
    }
}

fn f() {}

fn g() {}
--- errors ---
error: expected `;`, found `while`
 --> ./tests/cases/syntax/err_missing_semicolon_before_refusal.aipl:6:5
  |
6 |     while (true) {
  |     ^^^^^

error: missing `;` before `g`: every statement ends with one, even an `if` or `match` whose value is unused
 --> ./tests/cases/syntax/err_missing_semicolon_before_refusal.aipl:7:13
  |
7 |         f() g();
  |             ^
